     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. I understand you want things settled before evening rounds. But 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum daily dose, and the reason you gave isn't a clinical one. Metformin has no added glucose-lowering benefit above about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day. The extra dose mainly adds risk of severe GI effects and lactic acidosis, which can be fatal, and a hospitalized patient may also get contrast, become dehydrated, or have a change in kidney function.\n\nI can do either of these right away:\n1. **Enter a maximum-dose order**, such as metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). I can also enter 1000 mg twice daily or another regimen you choose.\n2. **Enter the order with a note** asking for pharmacy review before the first dose. This only makes sense if there's a clinical reason for the higher dose that I don't know about. If there is, please tell me, and the note can include it.\n\nIf the goal is better glycemic control by this evening, other options may work faster, such as adding or adjusting insulin. That's your call and the team's. Which of these would you like?"
      }
     ],
